{"type": "message", "id": "fa4dff15", "parentId": "29bb2e23", "timestamp": "2026-10-11T16:12:37.982Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "process_0_2dcf692d", "toolName": "process", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "OPT []\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 liuyu liuyu 142703 Oct 12 00:12 /tmp/ks.pdf\n/tmp/ks.pdf: PDF document, version 1.6 (zip deflate encoded)\nKANSAS  GRADE 8  PUBLIC SCHOOLS\n\nOVERALL RESULTS\n• In 2022, the average score of eighth-grade students in Kansas was 272. This was not\nsignificantly different from the average score of 273 for students in the nation.\n• The average score for students in Kansas in 2022 (272) was lower than their average\nscore in 2019 (282) and in 2000 (283).\n• The percentage of students in Kansas who performed at or above the NAEP Proficient\nlevel was 23 percent in 2022. This percentage was smaller than that in 2019 (33\npercent) and in 2000 (34 percent).\n• The percentage of students in Kansas who performed at or above the NAEP Basic\nlevel was 61 percent in 2022. This percentage was smaller than that in 2019 (71\npercent) and in 2000 (76 percent).\n\nNAEP ACHIEVEMENT-LEVEL PERCENTAGES AND AVERAGE SCORE\nRESULTS\nKansas\n\nAverage Score\n\n2000\n\n24*\n\n2019\n\n43*\n--\nNOTE: NAEP achievement levels are to be used on a trial basis and should be interpreted and used\nwith caution. Detail may not sum to totals because of rounding.\n\nCOMPARE THE AVERAGE SCORE IN 2022 TO OTHER STATES/\nJURISDICTIONS\n\nAVERAGE SCORES FOR STATE/JURISDICTION AND THE NATION (PUBLIC)\nScore\n500\n\nAK\n\nME\nVT\nWA\n--\n0\n'00\n\nIn 2022, the average score in Kansas (272) was\nlower than those in 19 states/jurisdictions\nhigher than those in 9 states/jurisdictions\nnot significantly different from those in 23 states/jurisdictions\n\n'05\n\n'07\n\n--\nlunches, is not displayed. Black includes African American and Hispanic includes Latino. Race\ncategories exclude Hispanic origin.\n\n• In 2022, Black students had an average score that was 29 points lower than that for\nWhite students. This performance gap was not significantly different from that in 2000\n(43 points).\n• In 2022, Hispanic students had an average score that was 20 points lower than that\nfor White students. This performance gap was not significantly different from that in\n2000 (24 points).\n• In 2022, male students in Kansas had an average score that was not significantly\ndifferent from that for female students.\n• In 2022, students who were eligible for the National School Lunch Program (NSLP)\nhad an average score that was 27 points lower than that for students who were not\neligible. This performance gap was not significantly different from that in 2000 (24\npoints).\n\nNOTE: The NAEP mathematics scale ranges from 0 to 500. Results presented in this report are based on public school students only. Statistical comparisons are\ncalculated on the basis of unrounded scale scores or percentages. Score gap results for \"White,\" \"Black,\" and \"Hispanic\" presented in this report are based on the\n6-category race/ethnicity variable with data available starting in early 1990s. Read more about how to interpret NAEP results from the mathematics assessment at\ninterpret results. For more information and additional comparisons please visit the Nation's Report Card and NAEP Data Explorer.\nSOURCE: U.S. Department of Education, Institute of Education Sciences, National Center for Education Statistics, National Assessment of Educational Progress\n\nProcess exited with code 0."}], "details": {"status": "completed", "sessionId": "amber-canyon", "exitCode": 0, "aggregated": "OPT []\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 liuyu liuyu 142703 Oct 12 00:12 /tmp/ks.pdf\n/tmp/ks.pdf: PDF document, version 1.6 (zip deflate encoded)\nKANSAS  GRADE 8  PUBLIC SCHOOLS\n\nOVERALL RESULTS\n• In 2022, the average score of eighth-grade students in Kansas was 272. This was not\nsignificantly different from the average score of 273 for students in the nation.\n• The average score for stude